m when atoms share electron pairs, as in H2O and CH4.","difficulty":"medium","source":"claude-generated-ecat"},{"external_id":"ecat-chemistry-chemical-bonding-q306","subject":"Chemistry","subject_slug":"chemistry","chapter_slug":"chemical-bonding","chapter_title":"Chemical Bonding","statement":"Which type of bond holds atoms together in a metal lattice?","chapter_num":8,"node_kind":"topic","option_a":"Covalent bond","option_b":"Hydrogen bond","option_c":"Metallic bond","option_d":"Ionic bond","option_e":null,"correct_option":"c","explanation":"Metallic bonding involves a 'sea of delocalized electrons' shared among metal cations.","difficulty":"medium","source":"claude-generated-ecat"},{"external_id":"ecat-chemistry-chemical-bonding-q307","subject":"Chemistry","subject_slug":"chemistry","chapter_slug":"chemical-bonding","chapter_title":"Chemical Bonding","statement":"What is the shape of the methane (CH4) molecule according to VSEPR theory?","chapter_num":8,"node_kind":"topic","option_a":"Bent","option_b":"Trigonal planar","option_c":"Tetrahedral","option_d":"Linear","option_e":null,"correct_option":"c","explanation":"CH4 has 4 bonding pairs and no lone pairs on carbon, giving a tetrahedral shape (109.5°).","difficulty":"medium","source":"claude-generated-ecat"},{"external_id":"ecat-chemistry-chemical-bonding-q308","subject":"Chemistry","subject_slug":"chemistry","chapter_slug":"chemical-bonding","chapter_title":"Chemical Bonding","statement":"What is the molecular shape of water (H2O) according to VSEPR theory?","chapter_num":8,"node_kind":"topic","option_a":"Bent (angular)","option_b":"Tetrahedral","option_c":"Linear","option_d":"Trigonal pyramidal","option_e":null,"correct_option":"a","explanation":"Water has 2 bonding pairs and 2 lone pairs on oxygen, giving a bent shape (~104.5°).","difficulty":"medium","source":"claude-generated-ecat"},{"external_id":"ecat-chemistry-chemical-bonding-q309","subject":"Chemistry","subject_slug":"chemistry","chapter_slug":"chemical-bonding","chapter_title":"Chemical Bonding","statement":"What is the shape of ammonia (NH3) according to VSEPR theory?","chapter_num":8,"node_kind":"topic","option_a":"Tetrahedral","option_b":"Bent","option_c":"Trigonal pyramidal","option_d":"Linear","option_e":null,"correct_option":"c","explanation":"NH3 has 3 bonding pairs and 1 lone pair on nitrogen, giving a trigonal pyramidal shape.","difficulty":"medium","source":"claude-generated-ecat"},{"external_id":"ecat-chemistry-chemical-bonding-q310","subject":"Chemistry","subject_slug":"chemistry","chapter_slug":"chemical-bonding","chapter_title":"Chemical Bonding","statement":"Which type of intermolecular force is responsible for water's unusually high boiling point?","chapter_num":8,"node_kind":"topic","option_a":"Hydrogen bonding","option_b":"Van der Waals forces","option_c":"Ionic bonding","option_d":"Metallic bonding","option_e":null,"correct_option":"a","explanation":"Water molecules form strong hydrogen bonds between the O-H of one molecule and the O of another.","difficulty":"medium","source":"claude-generated-ecat"},{"external_id":"ecat-chemistry-chemical-bonding-q311","subject":"Chemistry","subject_slug":"chemistry","chapter_slug":"chemical-bonding","chapter_title":"Chemical Bonding","statement":"What is the hybridization of carbon in methane (CH4)?","chapter_num":8,"node_kind":"topic","option_a":"sp","option_b":"sp2","option_c":"sp3d","option_d":"sp3","option_e":null,"correct_option":"d","explanation":"Carbon in methane forms 4 sigma bonds with tetrahedral geometry, requiring sp3 hybridization.","difficulty":"medium","source":"claude-generated-ecat"},{"external_id":"ecat-chemistry-chemical-bonding-q312","subject":"Chemistry","subject_slug":"chemistry","chapter_slug":"chemical-bonding","chapter_title":"Chemical Bonding","statement":"What is the hybridization of carbon in ethene (C2H4)?","chapter_num":8,"node_kind":"topic","option_a":"sp3","option_b":"sp2","option_c":"sp3d","option_d":"sp","option_e":null,"correct_option":"b","explanation":"Each carbon in ethene forms 3 sigma bonds (trigonal planar) and 1 pi bond, requiring sp2 hybridization.","difficulty":"medium","source":"claude-generated-ecat"},{"external_id":"ecat-chemistry-chemical-bonding-q313","subject":"Chemistry","subject_slug":"chemistry","chapter_slug":"chemical-bonding","chapter_title":"Chemical Bonding","statement":"What is the hybridization of carbon in ethyne (C2H2)?","chapter_num":8,"node_kind":"topic","option_a":"sp2","option_b":"sp","option_c":"sp3d2","option_d":"sp3","option_e":null,"correct_option":"b","explanation":"Each carbon in ethyne forms 2 sigma bonds (linear) and 2 pi bonds, requiring sp hybridization.","difficulty":"medium","source":"claude-generated-ecat"},{"external_id":"ecat-chemistry-chemical-bonding-q314","subject":"Chemistry","subject_slug":"chemistry","chapter_slug":"chemical-bonding","chapter_title":"Chemical Bonding","statement":"An atom that loses electrons forms what kind of ion?","chapter_num":8,"node_kind":"topic","option_a":"Cation (positive ion)","option_b":"Anion (negative ion)","option_c":"Neutral atom","option_d":"Isotope","option_e":null,"correct_option":"a","explanation":"Losing negatively charged electrons leaves a net positive charge, forming a cation.","difficulty":"medium","source":"claude-generated-ecat"},{"external_id":"ecat-chemistry-chemical-bonding-q315","subject":"Chemistry","subject_slug":"chemistry","chapter_slug":"chemical-bonding","chapter_title":"Chemical Bonding","statement":"An atom that gains electrons forms what kind of ion?","chapter_num":8,"node_kind":"topic","option_a":"Cation (positive ion)","option_b":"Isotope","option_c":"Neutral atom","option_d":"Anion (negative ion)","option_e":null,"correct_option":"d","explanation":"Gaining negatively charged electrons gives a net negative charge, forming an anion.","difficulty":"medium","source":"claude-generated-ecat"},{"external_id":"ecat-chemistry-chemical-bonding-q316","subject":"Chemistry","subject_slug":"chemistry","chapter_slug":"chemical-bonding","chapter_title":"Chemical Bonding","statement":"What term describes atoms of the same element with different numbers of neutrons?","chapter_num":8,"node_kind":"topic","option_a":"Isobars","option_b":"Isomers","option_c":"Isotopes","option_d":"Allotropes","option_e":null,"correct_option":"c","explanation":"Isotopes have the same atomic number (protons) but different mass numbers (neutrons).","difficulty":"medium","source":"claude-generated-ecat"},{"external_id":"ecat-chemistry-chemical-bonding-q317","subject":"Chemistry","subject_slug":"chemistry","chapter_slug":"chemical-bonding","chapter_title":"Chemical Bonding","statement":"What is the term for different structural forms of the same element in the same physical state?","chapter_num":8,"node_kind":"topic","option_a":"Isomers","option_b":"Isotopes","option_c":"Isobars","option_d":"Allotropes","option_e":null,"correct_option":"d","explanation":"Carbon's allotropes include diamond, graphite, and fullerenes - same element, different structures.","difficulty":"medium","source":"claude-generated-ecat"},{"external_id":"ecat-chemistry-chemical-bonding-q318","subject":"Chemistry","subject_slug":"chemistry","chapter_slug":"chemical-bonding","chapter_title":"Chemical Bonding","statement":"According to the octet rule, atoms tend to gain, lose, or share electrons to achieve how many electrons in their outer shell?","chapter_num":8,"node_kind":"topic","option_a":"6 electrons","option_b":"2 electrons","option_c":"8 electrons","option_d":"10 electrons","option_e":null,"correct_option":"c","explanation":"The octet rule states atoms are most stable with 8 valence electrons, resembling noble gas configuration.","difficulty":"medium","source":"claude-generated-ecat"},{"external_id":"ecat-chemistry-organic-chemistry-q319","subject":"Chemistry","subject_slug":"chemistry","chapter_slug":"organic-chemistry","chapter_title":"Organic Chemistry Basics","statement":"What is the general formula 