ter_slug":"organic-chemistry","chapter_title":"Organic Chemistry Basics","statement":"What is the process called when hydrogen is added across a C=C double bond?","chapter_num":9,"node_kind":"topic","option_a":"Oxidation","option_b":"Hydration","option_c":"Hydrogenation","option_d":"Halogenation","option_e":null,"correct_option":"c","explanation":"Hydrogenation adds H2 across a double bond, typically using a metal catalyst, converting alkenes to alkanes.","difficulty":"medium","source":"claude-generated-ecat"},{"external_id":"ecat-chemistry-organic-chemistry-q331","subject":"Chemistry","subject_slug":"chemistry","chapter_slug":"organic-chemistry","chapter_title":"Organic Chemistry Basics","statement":"What is the product when an alkene reacts with water in the presence of an acid catalyst?","chapter_num":9,"node_kind":"topic","option_a":"Ester","option_b":"Alkane","option_c":"Alcohol (via hydration)","option_d":"Carboxylic acid","option_e":null,"correct_option":"c","explanation":"Acid-catalyzed hydration adds H-OH across the double bond, producing an alcohol.","difficulty":"medium","source":"claude-generated-ecat"},{"external_id":"ecat-chemistry-organic-chemistry-q332","subject":"Chemistry","subject_slug":"chemistry","chapter_slug":"organic-chemistry","chapter_title":"Organic Chemistry Basics","statement":"What type of reaction converts a carboxylic acid and an alcohol into an ester?","chapter_num":9,"node_kind":"topic","option_a":"Hydrogenation","option_b":"Oxidation","option_c":"Esterification","option_d":"Hydrolysis","option_e":null,"correct_option":"c","explanation":"Esterification combines a carboxylic acid and alcohol (with acid catalyst) to form an ester and water.","difficulty":"medium","source":"claude-generated-ecat"},{"external_id":"ecat-chemistry-organic-chemistry-q333","subject":"Chemistry","subject_slug":"chemistry","chapter_slug":"organic-chemistry","chapter_title":"Organic Chemistry Basics","statement":"Benzene (C6H6) is an example of which class of 