ct_slug":"chemistry","chapter_slug":"equilibrium-electrochemistry","chapter_title":"Chemical Equilibrium and Electrochemistry","statement":"What is the term for the minimum energy required for a reaction to occur?","chapter_num":10,"node_kind":"topic","option_a":"Ionization energy","option_b":"Lattice energy","option_c":"Activation energy","option_d":"Bond energy","option_e":null,"correct_option":"c","explanation":"Activation energy is the energy barrier reactants must overcome to form products.","difficulty":"medium","source":"claude-generated-ecat"},{"external_id":"ecat-chemistry-equilibrium-electrochemistry-q337","subject":"Chemistry","subject_slug":"chemistry","chapter_slug":"equilibrium-electrochemistry","chapter_title":"Chemical Equilibrium and Electrochemistry","statement":"What role does a catalyst play in a chemical reaction?","chapter_num":10,"node_kind":"topic","option_a":"It increases the activation energy","option_b":"It lowers the activation energy without being consumed","option_c":"It shifts the equilibrium position","option_d":"It is consumed in the reaction","option_e":null,"correct_option":"b","explanation":"Catalysts provide an alternate pathway with lower activation energy, speeding up the reaction without being used up.","difficulty":"medium","source":"claude-generated-ecat"},{"external_id":"ecat-chemistry-equilibrium-electrochemistry-q338","subject":"Chemistry","subject_slug":"chemistry","chapter_slug":"equilibrium-electrochemistry","chapter_title":"Chemical Equilibrium and Electrochemistry","statement":"In a redox reaction, what happens during oxidation?","chapter_num":10,"node_kind":"topic","option_a":"Gain of electrons","option_b":"Loss of electrons","option_c":"Gain of protons","option_d":"Loss of protons","option_e":null,"correct_option":"b","explanation":"Oxidation is defined as the loss of electrons (OIL RIG: Oxidation Is Loss).","difficulty":"medium","source":"claude-generated-ecat"},{"external_id":"ecat-chemistry-equilibrium-electrochemistry-q339","subject":"Chemistry","subject_slug":"chemistry","chapter_slug":"equilibrium-electrochemistry","chapter_title":"Chemical Equilibrium and Electrochemistry","statement":"In a redox reaction, what happens during reduction?","chapter_num":10,"node_kind":"topic","option_a":"Gain of protons","option_b":"Gain of electrons","option_c":"Loss of electrons","option_d":"Loss of protons","option_e":null,"correct_option":"b","explanation":"Reduction is defined as the gain of electrons (OIL RIG: Reduction Is Gain).","difficulty":"medium","source":"claude-generated-ecat"},{"external_id":"ecat-chemistry-equilibrium-electrochemistry-q340","subject":"Chemistry","subject_slug":"chemistry","chapter_slug":"equilibrium-electrochemistry","chapter_title":"Chemical Equilibrium and Electrochemistry","statement":"What is the pH of a neutral solution at 25°C?","chapter_num":10,"node_kind":"topic","option_a":"0","option_b":"14","option_c":"1","option_d":"7","option_e":null,"correct_option":"d","explanation":"Pure water at 25°C has [H+] = 10^-7 M, giving pH = 7, which defines neutrality.","difficulty":"medium","source":"claude-generated-ecat"},{"external_id":"ecat-chemistry-equilibrium-electrochemistry-q341","subject":"Chemistry","subject_slug":"chemistry","chapter_slug":"equilibrium-electrochemistry","chapter_title":"Chemical Equilibrium and Electrochemistry","statement":"A solution with pH less than 7 is considered?","chapter_num":10,"node_kind":"topic","option_a":"Acidic","option_b":"Neutral","option_c":"Basic","option_d":"Amphoteric","option_e":null,"correct_option":"a","explanation":"pH below 7 indicates a higher concentration of H+ ions, making the solution acidic.","difficulty":"medium","source":"claude-generated-ecat"},{"external_id":"ecat-chemistry-equilibrium-electrochemistry-q342","subject":"Chemistry","subject_slug":"chemistry","chapter_slug":"equilibrium-electrochemistry","chapter_title":"Chemical Equilibrium and Electrochemistry","statement":"A solution with pH greater than 7 is considered?","chapter_num":10,"node_kind":"topic","option_a":"Amphoteric","option_b":"Basic (alkaline)","option_c":"Neutral","option_d":"Acidic","option_e":null,"correct_option":"b","explanation":"pH above 7 indicates a lower H+ concentration relative to OH-, making the solution basic.","difficulty":"medium","source":"claude-generated-ecat"},{"external_id":"ecat-chemistry-equilibrium-electrochemistry-q343","subject":"Chemistry","subject_slug":"chemistry","chapter_slug":"equilibrium-electrochemistry","chapter_title":"Chemical Equilibrium and Electrochemistry","statement":"What device converts chemical energy into electrical energy through a spontaneous redox reaction?","chapter_num":10,"node_kind":"topic","option_a":"Capacitor","option_b":"Fuel injector","option_c":"Electrolytic cell","option_d":"Galvanic (voltaic) cell","option_e":null,"correct_option":"d","explanation":"A galvanic cell uses a spontaneous redox reaction to generate electrical current.","difficulty":"medium","source":"claude-generated-ecat"},{"external_id":"ecat-chemistry-equilibrium-electrochemistry-q344","subject":"Chemistry","subject_slug":"chemistry","chapter_slug":"equilibrium-electrochemistry","chapter_title":"Chemical Equilibrium and Electrochemistry","statement":"In an electrolytic cell, at which electrode does reduction occur?","chapter_num":10,"node_kind":"topic","option_a":"Cathode","option_b":"Anion","option_c":"Salt bridge","option_d":"Anode","option_e":null,"correct_option":"a","explanation":"By convention, reduction always occurs at the cathode in both galvanic and electrolytic cells.","difficulty":"medium","source":"claude-generated-ecat"},{"external_id":"ecat-chemistry-equilibrium-electrochemistry-q345","subject":"Chemistry","subject_slug":"chemistry","chapter_slug":"equilibrium-electrochemistry","chapter_title":"Chemical Equilibrium and Electrochemistry","statement":"What is Avogadro's number, the number of particles in one mole of a substance?","chapter_num":10,"node_kin